WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.741
[Caroline] This is "Share Your World,"

00:00:02.741 --> 00:00:04.176
the show where we share

00:00:04.176 --> 00:00:05.411
stories and reflect

00:00:05.411 --> 00:00:07.446
on our own cultural backgrounds,

00:00:07.446 --> 00:00:08.647
so that we can learn about

00:00:08.647 --> 00:00:10.549
each other's worlds from home.

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:13.652
In this episode, we're talking
about all things music,

00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:16.956
theater, and dance - from
traditional performances

00:00:16.956 --> 00:00:19.091
to iconic instruments and more.

00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:27.299
[Caroline] I've always
been interested in music,

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.935
theater and dance, even as a child.

00:00:29.935 --> 00:00:33.873
Last year was my first
trip to Armenia in Yerevan

00:00:33.873 --> 00:00:36.175
and it was so close to

00:00:36.175 --> 00:00:37.343
a place that we visited,

00:00:37.343 --> 00:00:39.945
called the Megerian Carpet Factory,

00:00:39.945 --> 00:00:41.513
where they weave carpets but

00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:43.382
they also integrate everything

00:00:43.382 --> 00:00:45.918
from the Armenian culture
for visitors to see.

00:00:45.918 --> 00:00:50.189
So they have traditional
garb, music, dance, and food.

00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:53.125
When I went, they put on a
little performance for us

00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:57.062
with traditional music,
and we have an instrument

00:00:57.062 --> 00:01:02.201
called duduk and it's made
out of the apricot wood.

00:01:02.201 --> 00:01:05.170
It's such a beautiful
sound and I think it was

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.174
just an overwhelming
experience being in Armenia

00:01:09.174 --> 00:01:12.111
and hearing that sound that
I grew up listening to,

00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:14.914
and that music I grew up dancing to.

00:01:14.914 --> 00:01:18.117
"Guys I am a ball of emotions today, really"

00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:19.785
[Katrina] So one of my favorite

00:01:19.785 --> 00:01:21.887
 instruments in Yoruba culture

00:01:21.887 --> 00:01:22.887
is the talking drum.

00:01:23.522 --> 00:01:25.891
And the really cool part
about the talking drum

00:01:25.891 --> 00:01:29.495
is that it mimics human speech.

00:01:29.495 --> 00:01:32.431
It literally sounds like someone's singing

00:01:32.431 --> 00:01:33.666
or someone talking

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.569
I believe it's one of the only instruments

00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:40.205
that has not been able to be translated

00:01:40.205 --> 00:01:43.042
to midi sounds or to be synthesized.

00:01:43.042 --> 00:01:46.111
When I went home for the
first time when I was 17,

00:01:46.111 --> 00:01:48.847
I got to see the talking drum used,

00:01:48.847 --> 00:01:51.817
and it was just very
powerful to experience it

00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:55.988
in real life, on the
continent, in Nigeria.

00:01:55.988 --> 00:01:57.523
I just love it.

00:01:57.523 --> 00:01:59.258
[Kasey] So last summer I was able

00:01:59.258 --> 00:02:01.493
to go visit my parents in Korea.

00:02:01.493 --> 00:02:04.096
They've always wanted me to go
to the Gyeongbokgung palace.

00:02:04.096 --> 00:02:07.866
It's majestic, it's architecturally
really strong and sturdy

00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:11.603
and there's a changing of the
guard ceremony every morning.

00:02:11.603 --> 00:02:12.771
And what you can do is

00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:14.506
on the outskirts of the palace,

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:16.241
you can go rent a hanbok

00:02:16.241 --> 00:02:18.777
which is a traditional Korean
outfit for three hours.

00:02:18.777 --> 00:02:22.081
And then you get to walk the
palace and kind of, you know

00:02:22.081 --> 00:02:25.084
feel like what it must
have been like back then.

00:02:25.084 --> 00:02:27.953
So it's kind of like an
immersive theater experience

00:02:27.953 --> 00:02:30.990
and it's wonderful to see
everybody dressed in costume.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.658
[Jason] I think I was
there at that palace.

00:02:32.658 --> 00:02:33.859
I'm pretty sure it's the
same palace that I went to.

00:02:33.859 --> 00:02:35.961
[Kasey] Yeah it's a pretty famous palace.

00:02:35.961 --> 00:02:38.864
It's kind of where everyone
goes when you visit Korea.

00:02:38.864 --> 00:02:41.033
So when I was visiting Korea

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:42.334
I stayed at the Westin Chosun Seoul

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.203
which is really close and easy to get to,

00:02:44.203 --> 00:02:45.704
to Gyeongbokgung palace.

00:02:45.704 --> 00:02:47.840
And I mean the rooms are spacious,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.475
the amenities, the lobby's gorgeous,

00:02:49.475 --> 00:02:52.077
and it's just so nice to
just be a tourist all day

00:02:52.077 --> 00:02:55.114
and then come back to luxurious hotel settings

00:02:55.114 --> 00:02:57.916
and just rest, relax, rejuvenate,

00:02:57.916 --> 00:02:59.551
and then go back out and just, you know,

00:02:59.551 --> 00:03:01.453
see the rest of Seoul.

00:03:01.453 --> 00:03:04.723
[Jason] So for Khmer
dance, for Cambodian dance

00:03:05.391 --> 00:03:07.326
it used to be performed

00:03:07.326 --> 00:03:08.594
in front of kings

00:03:08.594 --> 00:03:10.396
and queens and royalty,

00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:13.932
where people would be
dressed in similar outfits

00:03:13.932 --> 00:03:15.501
to the Cambodian wedding outfits.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:18.404
But they would be in
golds with like brackets

00:03:18.404 --> 00:03:19.772
and with crowns,

00:03:19.772 --> 00:03:21.974
but they would do special hand movements.

00:03:21.974 --> 00:03:24.443
[Katrina] So there's hand
movements that correlate

00:03:24.443 --> 00:03:28.547
with the dance and the hand
movements mean something?

00:03:28.547 --> 00:03:30.115
[Jason] So there's definitely a lot

00:03:30.115 --> 00:03:32.117
of movement and
storytelling and it's slow,

00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:33.952
like watching like a ballet

00:03:33.952 --> 00:03:35.621
where you could actually see the story.

00:03:35.621 --> 00:03:38.057
Which is a lot about
like life and balance.

00:03:38.057 --> 00:03:41.560
And I was never able to get it perfectly,

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.662
because it's really technical

00:03:43.662 --> 00:03:45.064
and you have to bend your fingers

00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:47.433
and they practice bending
their fingers as a kid.

00:03:47.433 --> 00:03:52.471
So they can bend it all the way back.

00:03:52.471 --> 00:03:54.239
I don't know if you can try it,

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:56.708
but it's really intense.

00:03:56.708 --> 00:03:58.043
Yeah, you get it.

00:03:58.043 --> 00:03:59.611
And it tells a story.

00:03:59.611 --> 00:04:01.713
[Debby] So Switzerland is very much known

00:04:01.713 --> 00:04:03.682
for the alphorn or in Swiss

00:04:03.682 --> 00:04:05.384
it's called 'alphorn,'

00:04:05.384 --> 00:04:06.384
which is basically the same.

00:04:06.385 --> 00:04:10.989
And it's used or used to be used back in the day

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:13.425
in the Alps and the Swiss Alps where, you know

00:04:13.425 --> 00:04:17.796
they have cows and sheep and
they used a horn to call them

00:04:17.796 --> 00:04:19.832
so that they would come all back together

00:04:19.832 --> 00:04:21.700
and go back to the stables at night.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.036
And it doesn't have any buttons

00:04:24.036 --> 00:04:25.904
or any strings or any openings.

00:04:25.904 --> 00:04:29.741
It's just a solid wooden horn.

00:04:29.741 --> 00:04:32.277
And I've never played it myself

00:04:32.277 --> 00:04:34.580
but I know you Caroline have.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.249
But I know it's very difficult to play it.

00:04:37.249 --> 00:04:38.150
[Caroline] That was difficult.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.386
But after practicing,
it's like, once you get it

00:04:41.386 --> 00:04:42.754
you just don't wanna stop.

00:04:47.826 --> 00:04:49.528
Debby you have to do it.

00:04:49.528 --> 00:04:51.930
You have to do it the next time you go.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.066
[Debby] Yes I want to next time,

00:04:54.066 --> 00:04:56.668
whenever I can go back home,
it's gonna be on my list

00:04:56.668 --> 00:04:57.669
for sure.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:00.005
- [Katrina] I actually
wanna go to Switzerland.

00:05:00.005 --> 00:05:02.508
I wanna go to all of your countries,

00:05:02.508 --> 00:05:05.144
but Switzerland I have ties to somewhat

00:05:05.144 --> 00:05:07.045
because my mom is half Swiss.

00:05:07.045 --> 00:05:08.947
My mom is half Nigerian.

00:05:08.947 --> 00:05:13.051
Yeah so she tells me stories all the time

00:05:13.051 --> 00:05:16.188
of like how she would go there for holiday

00:05:16.188 --> 00:05:18.524
when she was young, but
I've never been able to go.

00:05:18.524 --> 00:05:22.461
So hearing all of your
stories, I'm like, yes

00:05:22.461 --> 00:05:23.695
have to get there.

00:05:23.695 --> 00:05:26.565
- [Caroline] And if you're
like us, I know you can't wait

00:05:26.565 --> 00:05:29.034
until we can travel
again to experience these

00:05:29.034 --> 00:05:33.172
and other music, theater and
dance performances in person.

